  <info><![CDATA[## PURPOSE AND STATUS

An abstract model of low-balance-triggered cash-for-e-float rebalancing among
kiosk operators. Customer transactions and operator trades are separate processes.
All money units, thresholds, demand, and delays are illustrative, not calibrated.
The selected network is undirected and fixed during a run. Balances, demand,
participation, thresholds, and settlement rules remain illustrative in both modes.
No sharing means no peer swaps; it is not an estimate of current practice.

## NETWORK SOURCES AND PRIVATE LOCAL FILES

The code explicitly declares network-source and selected-market as globals.
Startup and Setup default to Synthetic random. These variables do not depend
on chooser widgets. The Network source button offers three modes:

Synthetic random: generates an undirected Erdos-Renyi network using the kiosk
count and density sliders. This mode reads NO local network files and works
when neither data/ nor synthetic-networks/ exists.

Observed mutual willingness: imports from the private data/ directory. An A--B
edge represents mutual Yes reports, NOT acquaintance or actual trading. The
exporter normalizes IDs, removes self/cross-market reports, collapses duplicate
directed reports using any Yes, and excludes nonrespondent alters. Missing reverse
reports create no edge. All supplied respondents are retained, including isolates;
unavailable roster members are not invented. Source and checksum are in the
private import audit. Raw survey workbooks remain outside the ABM repository.

Imported synthetic: imports a pre-generated graph library from the
synthetic-networks/ directory, using exactly the same loader and CSV schema.
No summary-fitting or advanced graph-generation code is needed in NetLogo.
The supplied ensemble has pooled primary networks and corrected hub sensitivity
networks: 20 draws per generator at each size from 2 to 24 kiosks. Four matched
market-bootstrap fits contribute equally to each size. IDs such as
pooled-n12-r03 identify generator, kiosk count, and replicate. Initial balances,
customer demand, and trading rules remain illustrative when these graphs load.

Use Choose local network after selecting either imported source, then Setup.
Synthetic catalogs with generator and n_nodes columns offer those filters before
the final graph choice. The chooser reloads CSV tables and caches them in memory
for repeated Setup calls. After editing library files, reopen the chooser or run
reload-network-library. Switching to Synthetic random clears this cache.
Private network IDs are not
embedded in model widgets or code. With no ID selected, Setup uses the first
catalog entry. Missing files when a library is loaded produce an explicit error;
the model never silently substitutes a random graph for an imported experiment.
Switch to Synthetic random to run without files. Kiosk count and density sliders
are ignored by both imported modes. The loaded-network monitor records the
catalog's network_type and network_id, not the current button selection alone.

This CSV importer uses desktop file operations. Publishing the CSVs on GitHub
does not make them load automatically in NetLogo Web. A browser loader would
need asynchronous Fetch requests plus CSV string parsing, or bundled NetLogo 7
resources. See the site's Synthetic network generation page for details.

Both imported folders have networks.csv (network_id,network_type), nodes.csv
(network_id,node_id), and edges.csv (network_id,source,target). Keep each
undirected edge once and include isolated nodes in nodes.csv. Additional catalog
columns may contain generation seeds and fit/provenance information. Use synthetic
IDs and identify synthetic graphs in network_type; do not label them observed.

data/ and experiment results/ are Git-ignored. synthetic-networks/ can be versioned. Never publish
survey-derived files or fitted synthetic libraries without an explicit review.
Local simulation outputs may contain private network IDs as well. Saving a world
or exporting an interface can also capture runtime information; keep such exports
in ignored local directories. Git ignore does not remove previously tracked files
or history; check Git before committing. See experiments/NETWORKS.md for local-file provenance.

Network sharing uses the loaded topology filtered by participation. All-to-all
sharing bypasses its edges but keeps its nodes. No sharing suppresses peer swaps.
Demand types alternate in stable node order and remain synthetic; wealth is
uniform. Total requests-per-tick does not automatically scale with imported node
count, so cross-network comparisons should consider demand per kiosk.

## CODE-OWNED PARAMETERS

All simulation parameters are explicitly declared in globals, including
target-balance-percent, low-balance-percent, and settlement-delay. They never
depend on interface widgets for definition. initialize-controls supplies defaults;
startup invokes it automatically. Setup also initializes defaults if needed.

Widgets use ui- prefixed bindings. The Setup button calls interface-setup, which
copies those settings to the simulation parameters before setup. Go and Step
copy only the live balance-label switch. Core setup/go never overwrite parameters
from UI values, so headless experiments keep their own settings. For code-only
or API use, call initialize-controls once, set parameter overrides, then setup/go.
The batch harness does this automatically. Parameters survive clear-all in setup.

## QUICK START AND CONTROLS

Choose a source with Network source, then sharing-mode and Settlement delay
(ticks). Press Setup, then Go / stop or
+100 ticks. Step advances one tick so you can inspect pending trades. Compare
runs using the same seed and other controls. Setup clears
all histories and pending trades. Keep controls fixed throughout a comparison;
only show-balances? is intended as a live display control (updates next tick).

Low balance (%) is the trigger; Refill target (%) is the desired holding of the
scarce resource. Both are percentages of that kiosk's booked cash + e-float,
not percentages of its starting cash alone. Default: trigger 25%, target 50%.
The target must exceed the trigger. With starting-balance 500, total holdings
are 1000, the trigger is 250, and the target is 500. Equality at the trigger
does not initiate a trade. A trigger of zero disables initiation.

minimum-reserve is an absolute amount protected on BOTH outgoing legs when
booking a trade. It restricts trades, not customer service. Each kiosk starts
with starting-balance cash AND starting-balance e-float. model-seed reproduces
topology, participation, and customer demand. Network, matching, transfer agents,
and display operations use local randomness; paired demand stays identical when
sharing mode, delay, thresholds, reserves, or topology change, holding demand
settings, kiosk count, and seed fixed. Each demand batch uses a separate seed
derived from model-seed and the tick, independently of the trading process.

## EXACT TICK ORDER AND SETTLEMENT

At tick t, each Go step performs these phases:
1. Settle every previously booked trade whose due tick is t or earlier.
2. In random kiosk order, assess available balances and book new trades.
   Newly booked zero-delay trades settle immediately in this phase.
3. Generate and serve the customer requests for this tick, in sequence.
4. Check accounting, record end-of-tick low balances, update the view, advance time.

A trade initiated at t is due at t + settlement-delay. Delay 0 can supply the
current customer batch. Delay 1 leaves it pending throughout the current batch
and settles before the next batch. Delay 2 leaves it pending for two batches.
Pending arrows can show a due tick equal to the displayed counter: the displayed
counter advances after processing, and that trade settles at the next Go step.

## LOW-BALANCE TRADING RULE

At the rebalancing phase, a participating kiosk without a pending trade checks
whether available cash or available e-float is strictly below its trigger. It
requests enough of the low resource to reach the target, paying equal value in
the opposite resource. Customer requests never initiate trades or wait for one.

Among eligible, uncommitted participants with surplus of the needed resource
above minimum-reserve, choose one partner uniformly at random. Network sharing
requires a relationship link; All-to-all sharing allows any participating pair;
No sharing books none. Participation is independently drawn with probability
participation-percent / 100, not a guaranteed exact fraction of kiosks.
In synthetic mode, each possible relationship has probability network-density.
Imported modes instead use the fixed links from their local graph library.

Trade size is the smallest of: desired refill, requester's spendable opposite
resource above minimum-reserve, and partner's spendable requested resource above
minimum-reserve. Partial refills are allowed. If no eligible supplier exists or
the requester cannot pay while protecting its reserve, the attempt is unmet.
One kiosk can be in at most one pending trade and book at most one new trade
per tick in either role. The latter prevents instant back-and-forth matching.
A kiosk may settle an old trade and book one new trade in the same tick.

Matching assumes perfect knowledge of eligible partners' available balances.
There are no fees, travel agents, default, credit, external top-ups, provider
restrictions, learning, or relationship changes. Delay is a fixed experiment
parameter, not an observed travel time. Settlement is an atomic, equal-value swap.

## BOOKED, RESERVED, AND AVAILABLE BALANCES

Booked cash and e-float remain on their owners' ledgers until settlement.
Booking reserves the requester's outgoing resource and the partner's outgoing
resource. Available = booked minus reserved. Customers cannot spend reserved
funds, and anticipated incoming funds are not available. Reservations can
therefore cause a customer failure even when booked resources appear sufficient.

Settlement releases both reservations, debits each outgoing leg, and credits
its counterparty simultaneously. Reserved amounts are subsets of booked balances,
not additional money. Each pending quantity creates one cash reservation and
one equal e-float reservation. Swaps preserve both market totals and each kiosk's
cash + e-float. Successful customers convert one kiosk resource to the other.
check-accounting runs every tick to catch negative/overcommitted balances,
unreconciled market resources, and mismatches between trades and reservations.

## CUSTOMER DEMAND

One tick is an abstract batch, not a measured minute or day. Each of
requests-per-tick requests selects a kiosk uniformly and an integer amount
uniformly from 1 through maximum-request. Alternating kiosks use withdrawal
probability withdrawal-share + demand-polarization or the corresponding difference,
clipped to [0,1]. withdrawal-share is the midpoint; clipping may change the
market mean; odd-sized markets also have one more kiosk of the first type.
Alternating types around the circle are not geographic clusters.

A deposit adds cash and removes equal e-float. A withdrawal does the reverse.
The requested outgoing resource must be fully available or the request fails
and is lost. There is no partial customer service, retry, queue, persistent
customer, spending feedback, common shock, or insufficient customer funds.

## VISUALS AND OUTPUT DEFINITIONS

Orange kiosks have low available cash; blue have low available e-float; teal
are above both triggers. Small nodes do not participate. A dark-centered ring
marks an operator with a pending trade. Labels show AVAILABLE cash / e-float.
Positions around the circle are illustrative, with no distance interpretation.
Network mode shows eligible relationship links; teal links settled this tick.
Amber arrows show pending trades from requester to partner, NOT the direction
of cash movement. Their labels show amount @ due tick. Both legs settle together.
Pending arrows also appear in all-to-all mode; zero-delay trades leave none.

Value served (%) is completed customer value / requested value * 100. Deposit
and withdrawal lines have separate denominators; before demand, rates display
zero as a placeholder. Customer demand shows served and failed value this tick.
These flows are not GDP, economic spending, or unique money.

The trade pipeline counts bilateral trades started and settled; the difference
is pending. Reserved cash and Reserved e-float monitors show commitments (equal
in this model). Unmet attempts counts low-balance checks that could book no
positive trade, not customers or unique operators. A partial refill counts as a
successful booking even if the target remains unmet. settled-value counts the
cash leg once, not the sum of both legs. No per-request rescue attribution is made.

The low-balance plot is an end-of-tick count, using the configurable trigger.
cash-low-ticks and efloat-low-ticks accumulate these kiosk-tick observations.
Divide by count kiosks * ticks to obtain the share of observed kiosk-ticks
below each threshold after at least one tick. They are not weekly shortage events.

## EXPERIMENTS AND LIMITATIONS

Compare the three sharing modes at delays 0, 1, and 2 with paired seeds. Then
vary the trigger, refill target, and reserve to see whether earlier initiation
offsets delay and how commitments constrain service. Test sparse networks and
partial participation. With withdrawal-share 1 and demand-polarization 0,
peer swaps cannot solve a sustained market-wide cash shortage.

No guaranteed monotonic benefit is assumed. Matching and reservations affect
which customers can be served later. Repeat across seeds. Before calling any
scenario current practice, add evidence-based external rebalancing and distinguish
reported borrowing from these swaps. The experiment harness and example sensitivity designs are documented in README.md.
]]></info>
